# Example requests for the running docker compose stack.
# VS Code: install "REST Client" and click "Send Request" above a request.
# JetBrains IDEs: click the run icon in the gutter.
# Requests that start with "# @name" store their response; later requests
# reuse ids from it ({{create.response.body.id}}). Run them top to bottom.

@base = http://localhost:8080/api/v1
@key = dev-api-key-change-me

### Create a high-priority SMS
# @name create
POST {{base}}/notifications
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "channel": "sms",
  "recipient": "+905551234567",
  "content": "Your verification code is 4821",
  "priority": "high",
  "metadata": { "user_id": "42" }
}

### Its status and delivery attempts (sent within a second)
GET {{base}}/notifications/{{create.response.body.id}}
X-API-Key: {{key}}

### Same request again with an Idempotency-Key: the second call replays the first response
POST {{base}}/notifications
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Idempotency-Key: demo-order-42
Content-Type: application/json

{ "channel": "email", "recipient": "ada@example.com", "subject": "Receipt", "content": "Thanks for order 42." }

### A batch (all-or-nothing validation, up to 1,000 items)
# @name batch
POST {{base}}/notifications/batch
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "metadata": { "campaign": "black-friday" },
  "items": [
    { "channel": "sms", "recipient": "+905551234567", "content": "50% off today only" },
    { "channel": "email", "recipient": "ada@example.com", "subject": "Black Friday", "content": "50% off today only" },
    { "channel": "push", "recipient": "device-token-1", "subject": "Black Friday", "content": "50% off today only", "priority": "low" }
  ]
}

### Batch progress: counts per status
GET {{base}}/batches/{{batch.response.body.batch_id}}
X-API-Key: {{key}}

### The batch's notifications
GET {{base}}/batches/{{batch.response.body.batch_id}}/notifications?limit=10
X-API-Key: {{key}}

### Validation: every problem reported at once, by field
POST {{base}}/notifications/batch
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "items": [
    { "channel": "sms", "recipient": "05551234567", "content": "missing country code" },
    { "channel": "email", "recipient": "not-an-email", "content": "and no subject" },
    { "channel": "fax", "recipient": "x", "content": "unknown channel" }
  ]
}

### Scheduled for later, with a signed status callback (the mock provider verifies it)
# @name scheduled
POST {{base}}/notifications
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "channel": "push",
  "recipient": "device-token-1",
  "subject": "Reminder",
  "content": "Your appointment is tomorrow at 10:00",
  "scheduled_at": "2030-01-01T09:00:00Z",
  "callback_url": "http://mockprovider:9000/callbacks"
}

### Cancel it before it is due (a callback "notification.cancelled" follows)
POST {{base}}/notifications/{{scheduled.response.body.id}}/cancel
X-API-Key: {{key}}

### Callbacks the mock provider received, with verified signatures
GET http://localhost:9000/admin/callbacks

### Create a template
# Template placeholders use {{ }} like this file's own variables, so the
# braces are written as JSON escapes ({ = "{", } = "}"): the
# API receives "Order {{.order_id}} has shipped".
# @name template
POST {{base}}/templates
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "order-shipped-{{$timestamp}}",
  "channel": "email",
  "subject": "Order {{.order_id}} has shipped",
  "body": "Hi {{.name}}, your order {{.order_id}} is on its way."
}

### Preview it
POST {{base}}/templates/{{template.response.body.id}}/preview
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "template_vars": { "name": "Ada", "order_id": "A-1042" } }

### Send with it
POST {{base}}/notifications
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "channel": "email",
  "recipient": "ada@example.com",
  "template_id": "{{template.response.body.id}}",
  "template_vars": { "name": "Ada", "order_id": "A-1042" }
}

### List with filters (then pass next_cursor as &cursor=…)
GET {{base}}/notifications?channel=sms&priority=high&limit=20
X-API-Key: {{key}}

### Live metrics: queue depth, success rates, latency percentiles
GET {{base}}/metrics
X-API-Key: {{key}}

### ---- Failure handling --------------------------------------------------

### Make the provider fail every call (503)
PUT http://localhost:9000/admin/config
Content-Type: application/json

{ "failure_rate": 1.0, "latency": "20ms" }

### ...send a few SMS now (first request above, several times): after ~20
### failures the sms breaker opens; they wait in "pending" without spending
### attempts. Worker readiness turns "degraded".

### Recover the provider: within 30 s the breaker probes, closes, and they go out
PUT http://localhost:9000/admin/config
Content-Type: application/json

{ "failure_rate": 0, "latency": "20ms" }

### Dead-letter queue: count what failed with 503 (dry run)
POST {{base}}/notifications/retry-failed
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "error_code": "http_503", "dry_run": true }

### ...and redrive it
POST {{base}}/notifications/retry-failed
X-API-Key: {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "error_code": "http_503" }

### What the provider saw: per-channel peak per second, duplicates
GET http://localhost:9000/admin/stats
